{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of J4 source readiness at head 77284b973d898d1846b7071ace6e398953d9f7c2, tree a392eeeb, for request 0c3091fa and review request aead7f40. Verdict: CHANGES REQUESTED.\n\nThe head adds six commits to main 31780874 and changes 55 paths. I read the implementation note first, then the diff of every supporting path, against rulings adb1a6ed, 241248a9, 1d0ffcab and 458166da and section 7 of `docs/kernel.md`. Two independent reads ran beside mine: one of the kernel packages, and one of the Scope adapter and credential custody. I confirmed each finding below in the code before using it.\n\nThe kernel change is sound. `hold@1` derives exactly as at main, the version dispatch never crosses families, and the `head` and `fork` rules follow ruling adb1a6ed. The defects are in the Scope adapter and its evidence. No path that I found leaks a credential or grants a wider one. The credential route, however, has no test, its custody owner has no security seal, and a fork's setup credential can stay live with nothing left to revoke it.\n\n## Changes requested\n\n1. No test covers the credential route.\n   - `WorkspaceHost.credential` (`packages/scope/src/workspace-host.ts:135-151`) is the only path that hands a workspace credential to a holder. No test calls it, and no test reads `private_workspace_token`. The J4 scenario `j4-workspace.scope.test.ts` labels its provider and credentials as stand-ins.\n   - The note's custody claims therefore rest on reading the code alone. These claims are the refusals for a wrong preparing key, a wrong holder, an ended hold or past instance, a fork that is not selected or whose ID and name do not match, and a second read after the one-time handoff. They also include the lifetime caps: 900 seconds for a workspace token, 120 seconds for fork-read and staging tokens, and the remaining hold time (`workspace-host.ts:110`).\n   - Fix: add a scope test that shows each refusal and the caps against the real `WorkspaceHost` and its SQL custody. A stand-in provider is enough. Add a control that removes the preparing-key check and fails the wrong-key assertion.\n\n2. The custody owner has no security seal, and the security note says it does.\n   - `scripts/security-entry-points.test.mjs` seals the existing custody owners `destination-host.ts` (line 231) and `site/host.ts` (line 210). The head adds seals for its callers: `object.ts`, `worker.ts`, `namespace.ts`, `scope-index.ts`, `artifacts-host.ts` and `artifacts-wiring.ts`. It adds none for `workspace-host.ts`, `workspace-lineage.ts` or `native-birth.ts`.\n   - The new paragraph in `docs/security/entry-points.md` says that the owners of \"this discovery, wiring and custody branch\" were reviewed and their seals adopted. That is not true for the custody owner. A later change to the actor, holder or expiry checks in `workspace-host.ts` would not trip the entry-point gate.\n   - The `scope-index.ts` seal comment still reads \"existing internal lookup/registration behavior retained\", although the head adds the `nativeBirthByName` gate there.\n   - Fix: seal the three owners with their maintained parser values. Then correct the paragraph and the comment.\n\n3. A fork's setup credential can stay live with nothing to revoke it.\n   - `workspaceFork` (`packages/scope/src/artifacts-host.ts:139-174`) holds the fork's initial write credential in plaintext under `workspace:\u003cname\u003e`. If its revocation does not confirm, the answer becomes unknown (`artifacts-host.ts:171`).\n   - Nothing then owes that revocation. No record names the handle, no code calls `revokeCredential` with a `workspace:` handle, and an unknown fork answer opens no deletion. The new case in `artifacts-host.test.ts` asserts that the plaintext stays held.\n   - Scenario: the provider creates the fork and the revoke call times out. A live write credential for an orphan fork then stays in custody, and each fork opening can leave up to three. It is never handed to anyone.\n   - Repository creation handles the same case differently. It returns the held credential handle with the answer (`artifacts-host.ts:275`), so a later revoke operation can find it.\n   - Fix: owe that revocation, or take the question to the planner. If the planner leaves it to later work, `docs/hosts.md` and the note name the live setup credential as an open limit, with the request that owns it.\n\n4. The kernel-gate text does not name all of the kernel additions.\n   - Section 7 counts any new file or export in `packages/contract` or `packages/derive` as an addition to the kernel. The head adds:\n     - a `hold@2` entry to `CAPABILITIES` (`packages/contract/src/capability.ts:104`);\n     - the derive exports `HOLD_V2` and `holdCapabilityV2` (`hold.ts:47` and `:1119`);\n     - `workspaceOwner` and `hasAnyWorkspace` (`packages/derive/src/capability/workspace.ts:10` and `:20`).\n   - The new section says that the head \"adds no capability name\" (`docs/kernel.md:261`). That holds for the bare name `hold`. It does not tell a reader that the head adds a declared capability version and four exports.\n   - Fix: name these additions in the section, and say in a sentence why each one belongs in the kernel.\n\n5. The manifest does not follow ruling 4e199bd8.\n   - That ruling applies to later heads. It asks the manifest to list each file the note cites, and to name each directory that the note cites whole once, with its file count, byte total and the SHA-256 of its `SHA256SUMS`.\n   - This manifest lists all 1,471 files. At 326,560 bytes it also exceeds the 40,960-byte limit of the gate's `notes` step, which warns on it.\n   - Every listed file verifies, so this is a matter of form, not of evidence.\n   - Fix: recut the manifest under 4e199bd8. The retained directory stays unchanged.\n\nItems 1 to 3 change test and production source. They fall outside the documents-only rule and the isolated-test exception in `docs/testing.md`, which covers only the repair of a failed test file. A new full gate must therefore run at the final head. The failed rows stay as they are: gate C at 40230449, the continuation at E, gate F at 9d775412 and the unclean diagnostic at F. The follow-ups at R stay separate rows.\n\n## Against the request and its rulings\n\n1. Ruling adb1a6ed, the kernel rule. Resolved apart from item 4.\n   - `hold@1` is the main body wrapped unchanged in `holdOwner(HOLD, false)`. With v2 off, `OPERATION_RULES` is the base set (`hold.ts:982`), so v1 still has no `head` or `fork` rules. Maxima, step code, reservations and error strings match main. The v1 fork record gains no field, and `forms-hold-v2.test.ts` pins its bytes.\n   - The family comes only from the declared version. `workspaceOwner` returns null for any other version. An unknown version fails validation. A v1 consumer ignores v2 check records, and a v2 consumer refuses v1 ones.\n   - `head`: a confirmed answer records the seed commit and opens `fork` at the ledger's next operation number. A closed hold gives `failed` with `hold-closed`, and a null head gives `no-head`. Refused or unknown answers derive nothing, as `stage` does.\n   - `fork`: a confirmed, selected answer makes the fork `selected` with its ID and name. A refused answer opens the next attempt, up to three, and the fork fails when every attempt's last answer is a refusal. An unknown answer keeps the fork `creating`. The host puts the incarnation, hold, opening and attempt into each name, so no name repeats.\n   - A late, confirmed, unselected answer opens a deletion for that answer's ID and name only, and changes no record (`hold.ts:970`). Without that deletion a known fork would leak, so it is needed for the two rules to be sound.\n   - The stricter v2 carried check needs the full FactRef and a confirmed `hold@2:check` outcome. It goes beyond the two rules, but it is sound and fails closed.\n   - Closure and effects: `head` has at most three effects, one operation and closure 42, which equals what the fork opening reserves. `fork` has at most two effects, one operation and closure 6, which equals one deletion. The ledger enforces both per outcome.\n   - The judge reads no clock, host state or caller label. Runtime and replay share one derivation path, and live-root retention follows the listed family. `packages/replay` is unchanged.\n   - `node scripts/check-kernel-boundary.mjs` checks 126 production sources with no refusals.\n   - Hold@2 rather than an extension of hold@1: the note and section 7 say that the registered-only census did not prove that no legacy outcome exists.\n2. Ruling 241248a9, sequence and scope. Resolved. The note states which J4 conditions the head proves locally: the two-holder Native scenario, with the provider, credentials and Git walk labelled as stand-ins. It also states which conditions wait for the live run after the deploy: two holders pushing to real forks, the old token failing after the hold ends, and publication from the chosen fork. The landing closes the source request only.\n3. Ruling 1d0ffcab, the page module. Resolved.\n   - The note names `npm run build --workspace @generalbusiness/dap-page`. Its retained run at F reproduces the committed bytes: 1,204,952 bytes, SHA-256 a859bd6b. The committed `assets.ts` has that SHA-256 at the head.\n   - The note explains that the page bundle imports the changed capability code.\n4. Ruling 458166da, the teardown repair. Resolved for this head.\n   - F to R changes only `join-version.scope.test.ts`, and R to the head changes only the note and the manifest.\n   - The R compile and whole-owner runs (receipts 88fcc43d and 74d0d569) exit 0 with the same head and tree before and after. Neither run's output contains the teardown error, while the diagnostic at F still prints it.\n   - The note says in two sentences what in the test outlived teardown and why production has no such path.\n   - Items 1 to 3 need a new full gate anyway, and that gate replaces this disposition at the next head.\n5. Existing pins. Resolved. `digests.ts`, `digests4.ts` and every existing definition file are unchanged. The J4 definitions match `J4_DIGESTS`. `pin.mjs --j4` writes only the J4 files. The J4 digests go at the end of the publishing and issue digest lists, and the test pins that order.\n\n## Checks at this head\n\n- The worktree is clean. `git merge-tree --write-tree` onto main 31780874 gives the head tree a392eeeb.\n- The 55 artifacts name exactly the 55 changed paths, all at this head, with the note first. Each reads stale only because it rests on question 0a0b8626, which the builder retired after reading ruling 241248a9. No artifact is itself retired.\n- I ran the owning files once each, with no whole suite:\n  - plain: `forms-hold-v2`, `forms-hold-dispatch`, `floor`, `git/host`, `j4-definitions` and `definitions`: 23 of 23;\n  - native: `workspace-lineage`, `artifacts-host`, `operation-before-send`, `j4-workspace.scope` and `join-version.scope`: 14 of 14, with no teardown error.\n  - The builder worktree stayed clean.\n- The evidence verifies. The manifest has SHA-256 de8109cc and lists 1,471 files. All of them match by size and SHA-256, 98,061,085 bytes in all. No file in the directory is unlisted, and no file or directory is writable. `SHA256SUMS` covers the other 1,470 files and reruns clean. The retained note equals the committed note.\n- The gate receipts agree with the table. Gate F (e721ddfa) exits 1 with complete capture and no dropped bytes. Its only failure is the Native `EnvironmentTeardownError`, with 853 cases passed and 2 skipped. Kernel, Node, Counting and Plain pass.\n- check-doc-links on an export of the head: 761 links, 0 broken.\n- check-notes from 31780874: one warning, on the manifest size (item 5).\n- check-commit-messages from 31780874: 6 checked, 0 flagged.\n- `git diff --check`: clean.\n\n## Points that do not block\n\n1. A confirmed fork answer that arrives after the hold closes opens its deletion and leaves the fork `creating` (`hold.ts:970`). `holdReserves` (`hold.ts:1003`) then reserves 42 entries for that fork for good. The `head` rule moves the fork to `failed` with `hold-closed` in the same situation, and the fork rule could do the same. Since a full gate must run anyway, this is a good time.\n2. v2 counts a fork creation twice: once in the open operation's closure and once for the `creating` record. The error is on the safe side, but v2 reaches its capacity refusal early.\n3. Refused fork attempts keep opening after the hold ends (`retries: () =\u003e true`, `hold.ts:950`). Only the host's held check stops the creation calls.\n4. One v1 validation message changes text: \"does not list a supported hold version\" replaces \"does not list hold@1\". No pinned data depends on it.\n5. The host records the expiry that the provider returns, with no check that it falls within the requested lifetime (`artifacts-host.ts:130`), and local custody trusts that value. Expired plaintext stays in `private_workspace_token` until a revocation confirms. It is never returned.\n6. Fork deletion and selection accept any valid repository name. They don't require the `h-\u003cscope\u003e-\u003cincarnation\u003e-` prefix that the host gives every fork name.\n7. The docs say \"an explicitly pinned `hold@2` lane\", but `object.ts` and `worker.ts` check only that the definition declares `hold@2`, not a J4 digest.\n8. A mint that the `ready` hook parks waits until another entry commits. The cost is liveness only.\n9. The note is hard to read. Many sentences are fragments or carry several claims, and it uses private shorthand: \"Root read it\", RAW, P, W, I and C(L).\n\n## Architecture\n\nThe kernel keeps the meaning of a head or fork answer, and the Scope host only supplies the answer. That is the split that section 7 and ruling adb1a6ed ask for, and `hold@1` stays fixed beside the new version. Lineage, custody and the provider adapter stay in `scope` and `git`. Approved, subject to item 4.\n\n## Security\n\nLineage binds the repository host, namespace and branch to authenticated records within fixed read and time limits. The route hands a credential only to the preparing key and the actual holder of a current hold, and only for its selected fork. The head never treats an unknown fork, stage or revoke answer as confirmed. The route that hands out credentials, however, has no test (item 1) and no seal (item 2), and a fork's setup credential can stay live with no revocation owed (item 3). Changes requested.\n\n## Simplification\n\nThe v2 rules reuse the existing forms, states and ledger, and the v1 body wraps unchanged. `workspace-host.ts` adds 263 lines for one provider. Approved.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n- Architecture: approved, subject to item 4.\n- Security: changes requested, items 1 to 3.\n- Simplification: approved.\n- The request's checks:\n  - These hold: the `hold@2` kernel rules, the preservation of `hold@1` and every existing pin, the version dispatch, the regenerated page module, the evidence integrity, and each failed gate row recorded separately.\n  - These are needed: items 1 to 3, each a source change with its own test or control, item 4 in the docs, item 5 in the manifest, and one new full gate at the final head.\n\nA head that makes these changes can come back for a review of its diff from this head.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:a1a030aa325751ec5f3f5320a6e89d629e071de8","binding":"assigned","head":"77284b973d898d1846b7071ace6e398953d9f7c2","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:4964f85b1ee183da8617159c6dd352b49c673456\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:710449ccd016033f1a6b40da348060ad7fe64c4a\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:a249511fd27df0e802098acbd392e6c466559302","review_path":"reviewguard@1","stale":"true","stale_bases":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sh